begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-004','[["Requirements Engineering value","A project team begins solution design from a brief verbal request and later discovers that different stakeholders expected different outcomes.","Establish a structured requirements engineering process before committing to the solution.","Continue design and ask stakeholders to accept whatever is produced.","Treat the original verbal request as a complete and unchangeable specification.","Focus only on technical features and ignore business outcomes.","Requirements engineering reduces ambiguity and rework by eliciting, analysing, documenting, validating and managing stakeholder needs."],["Stakeholder identification for requirements","Only the sponsor is consulted even though end users, operations staff, regulators and support teams will influence or use the solution.","Identify and analyse all relevant requirement stakeholders and plan how each group will contribute.","Consult only the person who controls the budget.","Wait until testing to involve end users and operations staff.","Exclude regulators because they are not part of the delivery team.","Requirements may come from multiple affected or authoritative stakeholders; missing them creates gaps, conflicts and compliance risk."],["Requirements elicitation technique selection","Users perform a complex process largely through tacit knowledge and struggle to explain every step in an interview.","Combine observation or job shadowing with interviews and process walkthroughs.","Rely on a single emailed questionnaire with no follow-up.","Ask the technical team to invent the current process.","Document only the formal procedure manual and ignore actual practice.","Observation helps reveal tacit work practices, while interviews and walkthroughs clarify purpose, exceptions and needs."],["Facilitated requirements workshop","Several departments have conflicting expectations and decisions are delayed by separate one-to-one discussions.","Run a facilitated workshop with clear objectives, decision rules, visual models and documented agreements.","Allow the loudest department to determine the requirements.","Collect separate wish lists and combine them without resolving contradictions.","Escalate every detail to the chief executive before discussion.","Facilitated workshops enable shared understanding, rapid conflict resolution and joint prioritisation."],["Prototyping","Stakeholders cannot visualise a proposed digital service from written descriptions and keep changing their interpretation.","Develop a low-fidelity prototype, obtain feedback and refine the requirements before full build.","Build the complete solution so stakeholders can see the final product.","Freeze the written requirement and prohibit clarification.","Replace stakeholder review with the developer''s personal preference.","Prototypes make abstract requirements tangible and help discover misunderstandings early at lower cost."],["Requirements analysis and decomposition","A high-level requirement states that the system must be ''fast, secure and user-friendly'' but gives no measurable meaning.","Decompose and refine the statement into specific functional and non-functional requirements with measurable criteria.","Keep the words unchanged because broad language gives the team flexibility.","Convert the statement directly into a project milestone.","Remove all non-functional requirements from the scope.","Analysed requirements should be clear, feasible, testable, unambiguous and sufficiently detailed."],["Requirements prioritisation","The requested features exceed the available budget and schedule, but stakeholders insist that every item is equally urgent.","Apply an agreed prioritisation method such as MoSCoW or value-risk analysis and document trade-offs.","Select features in the order they were mentioned.","Build the easiest items first regardless of value.","Mark all requirements as mandatory to avoid disagreement.","Prioritisation aligns limited resources with value, urgency, risk, dependency and minimum viable outcomes."],["Requirements conflict resolution","Compliance requires strong access controls while users request unrestricted access for convenience.","Analyse the underlying needs and constraints, facilitate a trade-off and document a compliant, usable resolution.","Accept the user request because convenience should override regulation.","Accept the strictest possible control without considering legitimate user needs.","Record both conflicting requirements as approved and let developers decide.","Conflicting requirements must be reconciled through evidence, constraints, stakeholder dialogue and authorised decisions."],["Requirements documentation","Requirements are stored in scattered messages, meeting notes and personal spreadsheets with no single approved version.","Create a controlled requirements specification or backlog with unique identifiers, definitions, sources and status.","Let each team member maintain a private copy.","Use meeting attendance records as the official specification.","Rely on memory because formal documentation slows delivery.","Controlled documentation creates a shared source of truth and supports traceability, design, testing and change control."],["Acceptance criteria and testability","A requirement says a report should load ''quickly'', and the testing team cannot determine whether the delivered response time is acceptable.","Define measurable acceptance criteria, including the required response time and test conditions.","Allow each tester to decide what feels quick.","Remove performance from the requirement because it is difficult to measure.","Accept the report if it eventually loads.","Requirements should be verifiable; measurable acceptance criteria establish an objective basis for validation and acceptance."],["Requirements traceability","During testing, the team cannot show which approved requirement a test case or design component supports.","Maintain a requirements traceability matrix linking needs to design, build, tests, changes and acceptance evidence.","Number test cases independently and avoid linking them to requirements.","Trace only requirements that have defects.","Use the project organisation chart as the traceability record.","Traceability demonstrates coverage, supports impact analysis and helps prevent omitted or unauthorised functionality."],["Requirements baseline and change management","A stakeholder sends a new feature request directly to a developer after the requirements baseline has been approved.","Log the request, assess value and impacts, obtain authorised approval and update the baseline and traceability records if accepted.","Implement the request immediately because it came from a stakeholder.","Reject it automatically because baselined requirements can never change.","Add the feature without updating schedule, cost or tests.","Baselined requirements may change, but changes require controlled impact assessment, authorisation and record updates."],["Verification versus validation","The specification is internally consistent and correctly formatted, but end users say the proposed solution will not solve their actual problem.","Recognise that verification passed while validation failed, and revisit the underlying stakeholder need.","Declare success because a well-formatted specification proves the right solution is being built.","Skip user validation and proceed to procurement.","Treat verification and validation as identical activities.","Verification asks whether requirements are specified correctly; validation asks whether they represent the right needs and solution outcomes."],["Requirements management through delivery","After approval, requirements are not reviewed again even though scope, risks, regulations and stakeholder needs continue to evolve.","Monitor requirement status, dependencies, changes, traceability and continued relevance throughout the life cycle.","Archive the requirements immediately after design approval.","Allow developers to modify requirements without stakeholder visibility.","Review requirements only after final acceptance.","Requirements management continues throughout delivery to maintain control, alignment and traceability."],["Aligning requirements with project value","A proposed feature is technically impressive but does not address a customer job, pain, gain, strategic objective or compliance need.","Challenge or deprioritise the feature unless a clear value contribution can be demonstrated.","Approve it because advanced technology automatically creates value.","Include it to use the remaining budget.","Measure value only by the number of features delivered.","Requirements should connect to stakeholder value, strategic outcomes and justified needs; unnecessary features increase cost and complexity."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
